# L §2.4 Expressions (tally 2.1.6, issue #81): a [ after an id touches it, whatever the id's word, so an item named
# added prints added[d], while a calendar's own list words keep their space (weekends [...], yearly [...],
# added [...]). tally 2.1.5's refcore took any added before [ for the calendar's word and printed def added [d in day]
# and added [d]; the M0 engine too (T's format vectors of households C and E2, LH-01 and I-9 hold those bytes).
=== options reprint
=== m.tally
model m
  tally 2
  on 2026-01-01
  start 2026-01-01
  end 2026-12-31

source s "s"

index day = 2026-01-01..2026-01-31

def len: calendar = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], yearly [01-01], added [2026-01-07]) at 2026-01-01

def added [d in day]: num = 1 at 2026-01-01

def two: num = sum(added [d] for d in day) at 2026-01-01
=== expect
--- m.tally
...
def added[d in day]: num = 1 at 2026-01-01
...
def len: calendar = calendar(weekends [sat, sun], yearly [01-01], added [2026-01-07]) at 2026-01-01
...
def two: num = sum(added[d] for d in day) at 2026-01-01
sha256 ...
